1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,150
One program reads tilt, voltage, touch and a button, and reports every second.

2
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:09,700
You've met the pieces. Now we read how they join.

3
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:17,396
Open 07 full dashboard reference. Check the five drivers, paste both files, and

4
00:00:17,396 --> 00:00:17,820
save.

5
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:25,620
Line 76 gives memory tools. Lines 77 to 80: board, pins, clock and text.

6
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:30,360
Lines 81 to 87 are one driver for each piece of hardware.

7
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:35,200
Switch on lines 129 to 150.

8
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:39,460
Line 129: updates per second, starting at one.

9
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:46,840
Line 131: the timer's flag. Line 132: the button's flag.

10
00:00:47,340 --> 00:00:54,780
Line 133 counts confirmed presses. Line 134 chooses the long or short display.

11
00:00:55,780 --> 00:01:02,340
Lines 136 to 141: when the timer rings, clear it and raise the tick flag.

12
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:10,520
Lines 143 to 150: when the button rings, line 145 clears it.

13
00:01:11,020 --> 00:01:17,020
Line 147 switches the button interrupt off. This is the first step against bouncing.

14
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:20,720
Line 149 raises the button flag.

15
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:26,760
Switch on lines 191 to 271. These are setup tools.

16
00:01:27,260 --> 00:01:33,154
Lines 191 to 196 name the voltage converter, its channel, and the timer channel for

17
00:01:33,154 --> 00:01:33,580
green.

18
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:43,280
Line 198 begins green dimming. Lines 203 to 205: channel four, low true, 20 percent.

19
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:47,540
Lines 207 to 209 choose a clock and set up the timer.

20
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:54,440
Lines 210 and 211 set 24,000 pulses a second. Line 212 starts it.

21
00:01:55,440 --> 00:02:00,160
Line 217 describes an input. Line 219 applies it.

22
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:03,700
Line 220: ring on a falling edge.

23
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:09,640
Line 221 gives the button priority one. Line 222 enables it.

24
00:02:10,140 --> 00:02:17,660
Lines 225 to 231 set red and blue as outputs starting at one, which means off.

25
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:27,060
Lines 233 to 241 set up the converter, with its reference, and without averaging.

26
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:33,770
Lines 243 to 258 take one reading: choose the channel, start, wait for done, and

27
00:02:33,770 --> 00:02:35,400
hand back the number.

28
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:40,780
Lines 260 to 265 prepare the periodic timer.

29
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:47,200
Line 266 sets the period: a million microseconds divided by the tick rate.

30
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:55,140
Lines 267 to 269 allow its interrupt with priority two. Line 270 starts it.

31
00:02:55,640 --> 00:02:58,280
Which is more urgent, button or timer?

32
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:03,780
Your turn. Pause the video.

33
00:03:03,780 --> 00:03:06,740
The button, because one is smaller than two.

34
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:10,940
Switch on lines 311 to 409.

35
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:18,622
Lines 311 to 315 name the sensor's mailboxes and expected answer. Line 317 lists

36
00:03:18,622 --> 00:03:19,520
addresses.

37
00:03:20,020 --> 00:03:28,767
Line 319 stores the address; zero means not found. Line 320 is one shared delivery

38
00:03:28,767 --> 00:03:29,300
form.

39
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:37,880
Lines 322 to 337 write a mailbox. Line 336 returns true on success.

40
00:03:38,380 --> 00:03:43,340
Lines 339 to 350 read mailboxes the same way.

41
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:49,060
Lines 357 to 359 set up I2C.

42
00:03:49,560 --> 00:03:55,880
Lines 361 to 374 try each address, stopping at the right answer.

43
00:03:56,380 --> 00:04:01,660
Lines 376 to 380 give a warning if it's missing, and carry on.

44
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:08,880
Lines 382 to 384: sleep, set range, wake.

45
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:14,580
Lines 394 to 400 hand back zeros if the sensor can't be read.

46
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:22,040
Lines 402 to 404 join the bytes and divide by four to drop empty bits.

47
00:04:22,540 --> 00:04:29,340
Lines 406 to 408 convert to milli g, using 488 thousandths per count.

48
00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:33,460
Switch on lines 449 to 501.

49
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:38,840
Lines 453 to 455 start the touch hardware.

50
00:04:39,340 --> 00:04:44,380
Lines 457 and 458 measure the untouched baseline.

51
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:54,960
Lines 461 to 476 scan one pad: choose it, start, wait, read, and clear the flag.

52
00:04:55,960 --> 00:05:01,400
Lines 480 to 483 subtract each pad's baseline.

53
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:06,220
Lines 485 to 493 turn negative noise into zero.

54
00:05:06,720 --> 00:05:16,237
Line 495: if the total is under 20, line 497 returns a special value meaning no

55
00:05:16,237 --> 00:05:16,960
touch.

56
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:22,340
Line 500 returns pad two's share, from 0 to 100.

57
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:26,600
If the changes are 20 and 80, what's the position?

58
00:05:27,100 --> 00:05:32,100
Your turn. Pause the video.

59
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:32,580
Eighty.

60
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:41,750
Part B. Switch on lines 533 to 539. Line 533 makes the touch memory; the rest wake

61
00:05:41,750 --> 00:05:44,540
the board and print a title.

62
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:51,680
Switch on lines 565 to 567: lights, dimming and button.

63
00:05:52,680 --> 00:06:01,201
Switch on lines 596 to 598. Keep fingers off the pads. Void in brackets on line 598

64
00:06:01,201 --> 00:06:05,000
means we choose to ignore the answer.

65
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:14,060
Switch on lines 623 to 625. The timer starts last, after every sensor is ready.

66
00:06:15,060 --> 00:06:18,820
Switch on lines 674 to 730.

67
00:06:19,320 --> 00:06:26,520
Line 674 loops forever. Line 676 checks the button flag.

68
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:36,300
Line 680 lowers it. Lines 682 to 684 wait for the bouncing to settle.

69
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:46,223
Line 686: is the button still down? If so, line 688 counts it and line 689 flips the

70
00:06:46,223 --> 00:06:47,120
display.

71
00:06:47,620 --> 00:06:51,140
Line 692 switches the button interrupt back on.

72
00:06:52,140 --> 00:06:56,140
Line 695 is a separate check, so both events can be handled.

73
00:06:56,640 --> 00:07:03,040
Lines 697 to 700 make boxes. Line 702 lowers the flag.

74
00:07:03,540 --> 00:07:09,700
Lines 704 to 707 read voltage, convert it, read tilt, and read the slider.

75
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:17,480
Lines 709 to 712 print the long line, showing volts as a whole part and two digits.

76
00:07:17,980 --> 00:07:25,820
Lines 714 to 721 print two dashes for no touch, or the position.

77
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:36,400
Line 723 ends with the press count. Otherwise line 727 prints only the count.

78
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:43,688
Build, load, open the terminal and press Reset. Then tilt, slide a finger from E1 to

79
00:07:43,688 --> 00:07:45,560
E2, and press the button.

80
00:07:46,060 --> 00:07:48,780
These values are examples. Your numbers will differ.

81
00:07:49,780 --> 00:07:56,100
On line 129, change one to five. The dashboard updates five times a second.

82
00:07:56,600 --> 00:08:03,503
On line 682, remove the waiting count, and press twenty times. Any double counts?

83
00:08:03,503 --> 00:08:04,440
Restore it.

84
00:08:04,940 --> 00:08:10,700
You've read an entire embedded program, line by line. That's a real achievement.

85
00:08:10,700 --> 00:08:11,420
Well done.

